[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | — | — | *(no rejection/error branch exists in the C source)* | — |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Generic-boundary rows (tested anyway)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because the C rejects nothing, every input is "valid" and every input has a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m defined result. The generic boundaries the task requires are therefore recast as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "the C must not trap and the Rust must reproduce the same defined value". The two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ways a C-to-Rust translation of this expression can actually *fail* are (a) a Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m arithmetic-overflow panic where C wraps, and (b) a division difference. Both are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m covered below. `panic = "abort"` is set for the release profile, so an overflow
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m panic in the Rust `.so` would abort the differential test process — a hard,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m visible failure.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
